Perfect — let me show you exactly where and how to insert Option 2 (the middleware that handles preflight OPTIONS requests) in your Program.cs.

Assuming you're using .NET 6+ (Minimal API style), here's how it should look:


---

Program.cs — Full Example with Preflight Middleware

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add services
builder.Services.AddCors(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("MyCorsPolicy", builder =>
    {
        builder.WithOrigins("https://your-ui-domain.com") // e.g., http://localhost:3000
               .AllowAnyMethod()
               .AllowAnyHeader()
               .AllowCredentials();
    });
});

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(/* your scheme */);
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();

var app = builder.Build();

// ------------------------------------------
// Preflight OPTIONS Middleware — ADD THIS
// ------------------------------------------
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    if (context.Request.Method == HttpMethods.Options)
    {
        context.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status204NoContent;
        await context.Response.CompleteAsync();
        return;
    }

    await next();
});

// ------------------------------------------
// CORS Middleware — BEFORE auth
// ------------------------------------------
app.UseCors("MyCorsPolicy");

// Now Authentication and Authorization
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

// Map endpoints
app.MapControllers(); // or app.MapFallbackTo... etc.

app.Run();


---

Why This Works

OPTIONS requests don't have tokens — so they must skip [Authorize].

The middleware catches them early and responds with a simple 204 (No Content).

CORS headers are sent properly because UseCors is before UseAuthentication.



